// Windows's `gcThreadToken` provider (#3420, unblocks #3412). See
// ../gcthread.bit for the full decision record: WRFSBASE-installed FS state
// was measured NOT to survive a Windows context switch (#3410/#3391,
// non-zero immediately before a deschedule, `0000000000000000` after), so
// this reads the TEB self-pointer at `gs:[0x30]` (`NT_TIB.Self`) instead — an
// OS-allocated value that exists before the thread's first instruction runs
// and that Windows itself restores on every context switch by construction,
// the same guarantee `GetLastError`/SEH/FLS/TLS all already depend on.
//
// x64 only — ARM64 Windows is out of scope for this port (epic #3322's
// Details: "Target is x86_64-windows only"), so there is no arm64 payload to
// carry here. `compiler/parserasm.bit`'s `result` directive still requires a
// nominal `arm64 <reg>` alongside `x64 <reg>` — that register name is never
// reached by codegen for an x86_64-windows build and is kept as `x0` purely
// so this asm block's shape matches its Linux/Darwin twins.
//
// `65 48 8B 04 25 30 00 00 00` = `movq %gs:0x30, %rax`: GS segment-override
// prefix (0x65), REX.W (0x48), MOV r64,r/m64 (0x8B), ModRM 0x04 (mod=00,
// reg=rax, rm=100=SIB-follows), SIB 0x25 (no index, disp32-only base), then
// the 0x30 displacement as a little-endian disp32. One instruction, no call,
// which is what satisfies this function's `@nosplit` contract.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_gc_thread_token") fn gcThreadToken(): int {
  return asm { x64 { 0x65, 0x48, 0x8B, 0x04, 0x25, 0x30, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00 } result arm64 x0 x64 rax : int }
}
